<v Mrs. Mattoon>[IRA LAUGHING]
<v Mrs. Mattoon>[艾拉笑]

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.820
<v Ira Glass>No, no one's going to be on TV. No, it's strictly radio.
<v Ira Glass>不，没人会上电视。不，纯粹是广播。

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.820
<v Mrs. Mattoon>OK. Yona Lu doesn't care what happens then.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>好的，那约娜·卢不在乎会发生什么了。

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.110
<v Ira Glass>What if it were TV?
<v Ira Glass>如果是电视节目会怎样？

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:36.030
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I think she'd want to be on, too. [IRA LAUGHING] Even though she doesn't-- I mean, radio doesn't do much for her because she doesn't talk.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我想她也会想上的。[艾拉笑] 尽管她不——我是说，广播对她没太大用处，因为她不说话。

00:11:36.030 --> 00:11:45.240
<v Ira Glass>All right. As you might imagine, not everybody in the family takes all this so lightly. Danielle's father was never too keen on this.
<v Ira Glass>好了。正如你可能想象的那样，并不是家里每个人都这么轻松地看待这一切。丹妮尔的父亲从不怎么喜欢这个。

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:51.000
<v Mrs. Mattoon>He was quite actually bothered by the whole-- he thought we maybe had a problem in the family.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>他其实很被整个事情困扰——他认为我们家里可能有问题。

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:51.670
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

00:11:51.670 --> 00:12:00.250
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Mm-hm. [AFFIRMATIVE] I mean, for a while there, we had two daughters that only communicated through a duck.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗯。[肯定] 我是说，有一段时间，我们家有两个女儿只能通过一只鸭子交流。

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.620
<v Ira Glass>Yeah. That period that you're describing, when do you mean?
<v Ira Glass>是的。你描述的那个时期，是指什么时候？

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:06.820
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I would say they maybe were 10 and 12 or 9 and 11.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我想她们可能10岁和12岁，或者9岁和11岁的时候。

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:09.550
<v Ira Glass>And they would only communicate through the duck?
<v Ira Glass>她们只会通过鸭子交流？

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:29.870
<v Mrs. Mattoon>Well, Danielle didn't pay a whole lot of attention to Ashley, but she paid quite a lot of attention to the duck. So if Ashley wanted to get Danielle's attention, all she had to do was rev up the duck.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>嗯，丹妮尔不怎么关注阿什利，但她对那只鸭子很关注。所以如果阿什利想引起丹妮尔的注意，她只需要发动那只鸭子。

00:12:29.870 --> 00:12:34.670
<v Ira Glass>And how long did this last?
<v Ira Glass>这持续了多久？

00:12:34.670 --> 00:12:46.880
<v Mrs. Mattoon>I can't remember. She'd also make Danielle laugh that way. Danielle thought Duki was very funny, but I can't remember her thinking Ashley was funny.
<v Mrs. Mattoon>我不记得了。她也会用那种方式逗丹妮尔笑。丹妮尔认为杜基很有趣，但我记不起她觉得阿什利很有趣。

00:12:46.880 --> 00:13:13.190
<v Danielle>In terms of the relationship between my sister and me, I don't know why-- I mean, this is probably completely, really sick-- but I have so much genuine affection and love for Duki that it's very easy, and it's very easy to demonstrate those feelings, in a way that it's not as easy to kind of demonstrate those feelings toward my sister. Just because we never got in the habit of it.
<v Danielle>关于我和妹妹的关系，我不知道为什么——我是说，这可能真的很病态——但我对杜基有非常纯粹的喜爱和爱，以至于很容易表达这些感受，而对我妹妹表达这些感受却没那么容易。只是因为我们从来没养成那样的习惯。

00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:18.440
<v Ira Glass>What percentage of your relationship with your sister is based on your relationship with Duki?
<v Ira Glass>你和你妹妹的关系中，有多少比例是基于你和杜基的关系？

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:49.230
<v Danielle>Well, the really fun part of it is based on my relationship with Duki. But I think as we've gotten older and older, we've gotten more and more self-conscious about the Duki factor in our relationship. But I think kind of a big chunk. I mean, it definitely kind of gives me this vision into her brain that I wouldn't have otherwise.
<v Danielle>嗯，非常有趣的部分是基于我和杜基的关系。但我认为随着我们越来越大，我们在关系中对“杜基因素”感到越来越不自在。但我认为占了很大一部分。我是说，它绝对让我对她的思维有了一个视角，否则我是不会有的。

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:56.690
<v Ira Glass>Well, I did finally snag an interview with Duki by calling Ashley.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我最终通过给阿什利打电话，争取到了对杜基的采访。

00:13:56.690 --> 00:13:58.780
<v Ira Glass>Is Duki still up for this?
<v Ira Glass>杜基还愿意接受采访吗？

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:02.900
<v Ashley>Yeah. He just got back from a party, though.
<v Ashley>可以。不过它刚从派对回来。

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:04.270
<v Ira Glass>He just got back from a party?
<v Ira Glass>它刚从派对回来？

00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:16.190
<v Ashley>Yeah. He was at a happy hour thing with a lot of college students. He's not in college, but he's in a band, so a lot of his friends go to this happy hour on Friday nights.
<v Ashley>是的。它和一个大学生去参加了一个快乐时光活动。它没上大学，但它在一个乐队里，所以它的很多朋友周五晚上会去参加这个快乐时光。

00:14:16.190 --> 00:14:18.560
<v Ira Glass>All right. Well, could you get him?
<v Ira Glass>好了。嗯，能让它接电话吗？

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:21.300
<v Ashley>Uh, sure. He's upstairs-- just a sec.
<v Ashley>嗯，当然。它在楼上——等一下。

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.210
<v Ira Glass>OK.
<v Ira Glass>好的。

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:24.120
<v Ashley>Here he is.
<v Ashley>它来了。

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:24.690
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Duki?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，杜基？

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:27.840
<v Duki>Yeah? Hey, Ira, how ya' doin'?
<v Duki>什么？嘿，艾拉，你好吗？

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.060
<v Ira Glass>I'm just fine.
<v Ira Glass>我很好。

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:29.680
<v Duki>Long time no see.
<v Duki>好久不见。

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:34.690
<v Ira Glass>Long time no see back at you. And welcome to our little radio program.
<v Ira Glass>好久不见。欢迎来到我们的小广播节目。

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.420
<v Duki>So what's going on here? You've got a whole bunch of celebrities on tonight?
<v Duki>今天晚上这儿有什么大人物吗？

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.870
<v Ira Glass>Well, we actually have a number of different people.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们确实有很多不同的人。

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:43.520
<v Duki>[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE] like Tom Cruise?
<v Duki>[听不清] 像汤姆·克鲁斯那样？

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.140
<v Ira Glass>They're just like Tom Cruise.
<v Ira Glass>他们就像汤姆·克鲁斯一样。

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:47.800
<v Duki>OK.
<v Duki>好吧。

00:14:47.800 --> 00:15:03.990
<v Ira Glass>Now, Duki, I was talking to Danielle for our radio program and had her come on and talk about you a little bit. And one of the things that she said was that when she was younger, in order to discipline her if she was doing something that you didn't like, you could pretty much control her with something called nose squeaks.
<v Ira Glass>现在，杜基，我为了节目和丹妮尔谈过了，让她来讲讲你。她说当你年轻的时候，如果她做了你不喜欢的事，你为了管教她，几乎可以用所谓的“鼻子吱吱声”来控制她。

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:19.260
<v Duki>Yeah. Because she has this kind of-- it's a prominent nose, you know what I mean? Kind of sticks out and you just want to squeak it. You know, like, over Thanksgiving we were watching The Muppet Show. And Miss Piggy was on, and she reminded me a lot of Niellie.
<v Duki>是的。因为它有那种——它的鼻子很突出，你知道吗？有点凸出来，你就是想捏它吱吱叫。你知道，感恩节时我们在看《木偶秀》。猪小姐也在，她让我想起了很多尼利（丹妮尔）。

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.250
<v Ira Glass>Of Danielle?
<v Ira Glass>丹妮尔？

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:27.180
<v Duki>Mm-hm. [AFFIRMATIVE] Yeah. And Kermit told Miss Piggy, "move the pork." And so I was telling Niellie to move the pork all week.
<v Duki>嗯。[肯定] 是的。科米蛙告诉猪小姐：“把猪肉挪开。”所以整个星期我都在叫尼利挪开猪肉。

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:28.460
<v Ira Glass>And would she move?
<v Ira Glass>她挪开了吗？

00:15:28.460 --> 00:15:30.670
<v Duki>Yes, she would. She would.
<v Duki>是的，她挪了。

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:39.740
<v Ira Glass>Now, if Ashley would tell her, if Ashley would sit down on the couch and say to Danielle, "move the pork," what would the effect of that be?
<v Ira Glass>现在，如果阿什利告诉她，如果阿什利坐在沙发上对丹妮尔说“把猪肉挪开”，会有什么效果？

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:57.550
<v Duki>Kind of-- you know Niellie. You know how she looks at you when she doesn't approve of something you say or do. She gets this kind of ice-cold stare and she gives you this sidelong glance that makes you kind of feel like you're about the size of a pea.
<v Duki>有点——你知道尼利。你知道当她不赞同你说的话或做的事时，她怎么看你。她会露出那种冰冷的眼神，斜视你，让你感觉自己只有豌豆那么大。

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:58.110
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.370
<v Duki>That's what she does.
<v Duki>她就是那样。

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:10.750
<v Ira Glass>Is there anything about the life of a duck that perhaps you could tell our radio audience that we might not know? I'm sure that you know much more about it than we do.
<v Ira Glass>关于鸭子的生活，有什么你能告诉我们的广播听众，而我们可能不知道的吗？我相信你知道的比我们多得多。

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:17.850
<v Duki>No, not really. I'm kind of an unusual duck. I'm not really in touch with the whole duck scene.
<v Duki>不，没那么特别。我是个不寻常的鸭子。我不太关注整个鸭子界。

00:16:17.850 --> 00:16:20.360
<v Ira Glass>You're not in touch with the whole scene, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>你不关注整个鸭子界，是吗？

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:43.580
<v Duki>When I had time, I used to migrate once in a while because I have some friends who are ducks. And I try to keep in touch with them, but lately I just started spending more time with people and doing my own thing. And I just don't have time to do those kind of duck things anymore. I just wanted more in my life than that.
<v Duki>以前有时间的时候，我偶尔会迁徙，因为我有些鸭子朋友。我试图和他们保持联系，但最近我开始花更多时间陪伴人类，做我自己的事情。我只是没时间再去做那些鸭子的事了。我的人生想要更多东西。

00:16:43.580 --> 00:17:18.800
<v Ira Glass>Duki, a stuffed hand puppet, now lives in New York City.
<v Ira Glass>杜基，一个毛绒手偶，现在住在纽约市。

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:29.070
<v Ira Glass>Well, the story of a 27 year old graduate student who talks like a duck naturally brings us to the story of Chickenman.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，一个像鸭子一样说话的27岁研究生的故事，自然让我们想到了鸡侠的故事。

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:59.410
<v Ira Glass>Chickenman first soared the radio airwaves from 1966 to 1969. Nearly every day there would be a new episode. These were these short little things, each one two minutes long or so. Starting on WCFL here in Chicago, but spreading to over 1,500 radio stations. Three times, by the way, that's three times the number in the public radio network. According to the people who syndicate Chickenman, it has been translated into German, into Dutch, into Swedish. It is still on the air, they say, in several dozen markets.
<v Ira Glass>鸡侠从1966年到1969年在广播电台风靡一时。几乎每天都有新剧集。这些都是很短的东西，每一集大概两分钟左右。最初在芝加哥的WCFL电台播出，但传播到了超过1500家广播电台。顺便说一下，这个数字是公共广播网电台数量的三倍。据鸡侠的联合制作人称，它已被翻译成德语、荷兰语和瑞典语。他们说，它至今仍在几十个市场播出。

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:24.990
<v Ira Glass>Chickenman. Chickenman existed years before National Public Radio existed as a national network. Chickenman will continue probably years after we're all gone. Like the mighty cockroach. Like-- I don't know. Like the bagel. Like [? Haleva. ?] Chickenman endures, will endure.
<v Ira Glass>鸡侠。鸡侠在全国公共广播电台作为一个全国性网络成立前就已经存在多年了。鸡侠可能会在我们所有人都消失多年后继续存在。就像强大的蟑螂一样。就像——我不知道。就像百吉饼一样。就像……鸡侠长存，并将继续长存。

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:30.040
<v Ira Glass>Well, let's hear what all the fuss was about.
<v Ira Glass>那么，让我们听听大家都在争论什么。

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:38.050
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>Now another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known.
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>现在是世界上最神奇的犯罪斗士生活中的另一个激动人心的剧集。

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:41.930
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>Bawk, bawk, bawk, bawk!
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>咯咯，咯咯，咯咯！

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:43.870
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>Chickenman!
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>鸡侠！

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:47.270
<v Chickenman Show Chorus>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!
<v Chickenman Show Chorus>他无处不在，他无处不在！

00:18:47.270 --> 00:19:11.630
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>Benton Harper, employed as a shoe salesman for a large downtown department store, spends his weekends, his only two days off, striking terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere as the white winged warrior called Chickenman. How did it come about that Benton Harper, weekend winged warrior, selected the visage of the chicken in his crusade against the forces of evil? Now it can be told.
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>本顿·哈珀，在一家大型市中心百货商店担任鞋类销售员，他利用周末仅有的两天假期，化身为名为鸡侠的白翼勇士，在各地犯罪分子的心中掀起恐惧。周末白翼勇士本顿·哈珀为何选择以鸡的形象来对抗邪恶势力？现在可以告诉你了。

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:12.100
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>[DOOR OPENING]
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>[开门声]

00:19:12.100 --> 00:19:13.860
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>Yes, may I help you?
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>是的，有什么可以帮您？

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:16.410
<v Benton Harper>How do you do? I'm looking for a costume.
<v Benton Harper>你好。我想找一套服装。

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:17.650
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>What did you have in mind?
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>您有什么想法吗？

00:19:17.650 --> 00:19:20.900
<v Benton Harper>Something that will strike terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere.
<v Benton Harper>能让各地犯罪分子的心中都感到恐惧的那种。

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:22.590
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>I see. Well, how about this?
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>我明白了。嗯，这件怎么样？

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:24.420
<v Benton Harper>Hmm. No, I don't think so.
<v Benton Harper>嗯。不，我不这么认为。

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:25.480
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>Why not try it on?
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>为什么不试一试呢？

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:25.960
<v Benton Harper>Very well.
<v Benton Harper>好吧。

00:19:25.960 --> 00:19:27.120
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>Here, I'll help you.
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>来，我帮你。

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:27.650
<v Benton Harper>Thank you.
<v Benton Harper>谢谢。

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:28.130
<v Benton Harper>[GRUNTING, PUTTING ON CLOTHING]
<v Benton Harper>[咕哝声，穿衣服]

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.530
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>There you are. Now take a look in the mirror.
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>好了。现在照照镜子看看。

00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:35.650
<v Benton Harper>Mm. Not bad. I wonder if you would permit me to conduct a quick experiment outside this store.
<v Benton Harper>嗯。还不错。不知道您是否允许我在店外做一个小实验。

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:36.910
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>Certainly.
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>当然可以。

00:19:36.910 --> 00:19:37.910
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>[FOOTSTEPS]
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>[脚步声]

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:42.900
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>[DOOR OPENING]
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>[开门声]

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:44.380
<v Benton Harper>Pardon me, sir.
<v Benton Harper>打扰一下，先生。

00:19:44.380 --> 00:19:45.080
<v Vicious Criminal>Yeah?
<v Vicious Criminal>嗯？

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:46.950
<v Benton Harper>Are you by chance a vicious criminal?
<v Benton Harper>你该不会是个穷凶极恶的罪犯吧？

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:47.880
<v Vicious Criminal>Uh-huh. [AFFIRMATIVE]
<v Vicious Criminal>嗯哼。[肯定]

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:50.210
<v Benton Harper>Fine. Would you take a look at this costume I'm wearing?
<v Benton Harper>很好。你看一下我穿的这套服装？

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:50.990
<v Vicious Criminal>Yeah.
<v Vicious Criminal>哦。

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:54.650
<v Benton Harper>Do you feel anything strange? Anything at all?
<v Benton Harper>你感觉到什么奇怪的吗？一点点也没有？

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:55.680
<v Vicious Criminal>Uh, yeah.
<v Vicious Criminal>呃，有。

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.140
<v Benton Harper>And what is that?
<v Benton Harper>那是什么？

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:59.450
<v Vicious Criminal>I'd like to kiss ya'.
<v Vicious Criminal>我想吻你。

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:00.470
<v Benton Harper>Kiss me?
<v Benton Harper>吻我？

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:01.150
<v Vicious Criminal>Yeah.
<v Vicious Criminal>是的。

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:02.800
<v Benton Harper>How do you account for that?
<v Benton Harper>你怎么解释这一点？

00:20:02.800 --> 00:20:05.230
<v Vicious Criminal>Because you look like an adorable bunny rabbit.
<v Vicious Criminal>因为你看上去像只可爱的兔宝宝。

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:07.170
<v Vicious Criminal>[KISSING SOUND]
<v Vicious Criminal>[亲吻声]

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.110
<v Vicious Criminal>[QUICK FOOTSTEPS]
<v Vicious Criminal>[急促脚步声]

00:20:09.110 --> 00:20:10.080
<v Vicious Criminal>[DOOR OPENING]
<v Vicious Criminal>[开门声]

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.320
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>Well, how did it go?
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>好吧，进展如何？

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:13.790
<v Benton Harper>What else do you have?
<v Benton Harper>你还有别的吗？

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:15.920
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>A teddy bear and a chicken.
<v Costume Shop Saleswoman>泰迪熊和鸡。

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.080
<v Benton Harper>A teddy bear?
<v Benton Harper>泰迪熊？

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:18.130
<v Vicious Criminal>Wouldn't it be cute?
<v Vicious Criminal>那不是很可爱吗？

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:20.740
<v Benton Harper>Wrap up the chicken, please.
<v Benton Harper>请把那只鸡包起来。

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:29.450
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crime fighter the world has ever known.
<v Chickenman Show Announcer>请收听明天世界上最神奇的犯罪斗士生活中的另一个激动人心的剧集。

00:20:29.450 --> 00:20:32.880
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>Bawk, bawk, bawk, bawk!
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>咯咯，咯咯，咯咯！

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.330
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>Chickenman!
<v Chickenman Show Chorus Member>鸡侠！

