<v Ira Glass>接下来，在胖鸡歌唱之前一切还没结束。稍后为您带来，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和国际公共广播电台，当节目继续。

00:24:54.120 --> 00:25:14.420
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme and bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, during this period of greatest poultry consumption in our nation-- the two weeks between Thanksgiving and Christmas-- we bring you stories of chickens, turkeys, ducks, fowl of all kind real and imagined.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《这个美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们都会选择一个主题，并为您带来各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目，在我们国家禽肉消费量最高的时期——感恩节到圣诞节之间的两周——我们为您带来关于鸡、火鸡、鸭子、各种真实和虚构禽类的故事。

00:25:14.420 --> 00:25:25.120
<v Ira Glass>We've done this poultry show so many times year after year in November that today's show is basically a greatest hits of chicken stories from many, many years past.
<v Ira Glass>我们年复一年在十一月做了这么多次家禽节目，以至于今天的节目基本上是我们多年来鸡类故事的精选集。

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:30.140
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our program. Act Three: Chicken Diva.
<v Ira Glass>我们到了节目的第三幕。第三幕：鸡影后。

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:41.810
<v Ira Glass>Chickens are what we make of them in a lot of ways. If you could possibly need further evidence of that after those first two acts we just heard, we have this story from Jack Hitt.
<v Ira Glass>在很多方面，鸡是我们赋予它们意义的产物。如果你在听完刚才那前两幕后还需要进一步证明，我们有杰克·希特带来的这个故事。

00:25:41.810 --> 00:26:03.060
<v Jack Hitt>Oddly enough, it wasn't Susan who was obsessed with chickens, it was Kenny, a pal who worked backstage at the 92nd Street Y in New York. His house was filled with chicken cups, chicken masks. He got the whole staff onto chickens, including Susan. For a time there in the 80s, poultry-related jokes and references became the fast way to get a laugh at the Y.
<v Jack Hitt>奇怪的是，痴迷于鸡的不是苏珊，而是肯尼，一个在纽约第92街Y中心后台工作的朋友。他的房子里到处都是鸡形杯子和鸡面具。他让整个员工团队都迷上了鸡，包括苏珊。在80年代的那段时间里，与家禽相关的笑话和引用成了在Y中心引起欢笑的最快方式。

00:26:03.060 --> 00:26:30.640
<v Jack Hitt>I guess most of us are condemned to see nothing more than the easy comedy of chickens, but Susan Vitucci saw something else-- their potential greatness, their hidden beauty, their grandeur. One day she glued together some finger puppets for a 10 minute rendition of the Chicken Little story for her nephew. That was 14 years ago. Today it is a full length opera enjoyed by a cult following whenever it goes up in a workshop or cafe or small theater.
<v Jack Hitt>我想我们大多数人都被注定只能看到鸡的简单喜剧，但苏珊·维图奇看到了别的东西——它们的潜在伟大、隐藏的美丽和壮观。有一天，她把一些手指木偶粘在一起，为她的侄子演了10分钟的《鸡丁》故事。那是14年前的事了。今天，它是一部完整的歌剧，无论是在工作坊、咖啡馆还是小剧院上演，都有一群狂热的追随者。

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:46.940
<v Jack Hitt>It's still performed with finger puppets, but now it has a complete score written by a noted composer, Henry Krieger, who did Dreamgirls. The Chicken Little opera he wrote with Susan Vitucci is called Love's Fowl. Needless to say, that's F-O-W-L.
<v Jack Hitt>它仍然由手指木偶表演，但现在它有了一位著名作曲家亨利·克里格（他创作了《追梦女郎》）创作的完整乐谱。他与苏珊·维图奇共同创作的《鸡丁》歌剧名为《爱的家禽》（Love's Fowl），当然，是F-O-W-L（家禽）那个词。

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:59.900
<v Henry Krieger>Well, we were going to start with the opening, Siamo del Teatro Repertorio delle Mollette. We are the Clothespin Repertory Theater, and we have a special singing guest for you, which I don't know--
<v Henry Krieger>好吧，我们要从开场曲开始，《Siamo del Teatro Repertorio delle Mollette》。我们是衣夹木偶剧团，我们有一位特别的演唱嘉宾，我不确定——

00:26:59.900 --> 00:27:05.960
<v Jack Hitt>Susan and I are sitting at Henry's baby grand piano. Henry's guest is his Maltese terrier named Toby.
<v Jack Hitt>苏珊和我坐在亨利的三角钢琴旁。亨利的客串嘉宾是他的马耳他梗犬托比。

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:08.340
<v Henry Krieger>Perhaps Toby would be kind enough to--
<v Henry Krieger>也许托比能好心地——

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:09.940
<v Jack Hitt>Yeah, would she sit on your lap for this?
<v Jack Hitt>是的，它能坐在你的腿上表演吗？

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:11.360
<v Henry Krieger>Yeah, no, yeah. Let's see what we can do.
<v Henry Krieger>是，不，是的。看看我们能做什么。

00:27:11.360 --> 00:27:13.670
<v Jack Hitt>OK.
<v Jack Hitt>好的。

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:19.840
<v Ira Glass>OK, listen carefully. Because once Toby gets going, he actually harmonizes with Henry and Susan.
<v Ira Glass>好的，仔细听。因为一旦托比开始，它真的会和亨利与苏珊一起和声。

00:27:19.840 --> 00:27:23.780
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - HENRY KRIEGER AND SUSAN VITUCCI SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 亨利·克里格和苏珊·维图奇用意大利语演唱]

00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:41.890
<v Ira Glass>[DOG WHINING AND HOWLING TO MUSIC]
<v Ira Glass>[狗随着音乐呜咽和嚎叫]

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:46.130
<v Ira Glass>You may have noticed that this libretto is in Italian, just like a real opera.
<v Ira Glass>你可能注意到这段剧本是用意大利语写的，就像真正的歌剧一样。

00:27:46.130 --> 00:28:21.390
<v Susan Vitucci>Before, it was just a bunch of puppets in a box, you know, with a good idea. And then suddenly, as soon as it went into Italian, it became something bigger than what it had been. And it's because when it's in English, we all kind of know it and it's really not that interesting. It's like, yeah, yeah, yeah. As soon as it's in Italian, it gives us enough distance that we can come in. You know, it makes us-- it's like the lover who doesn't want you. You don't want anybody more than you want the one who doesn't want you. Right? And so it's sort of the same thing.
<v Susan Vitucci>以前，这只是一堆盒子里的木偶，你知道，有一个好点子。然后突然间，一旦变成意大利语，它就变得比以前更宏大了。这是因为当它是英语时，我们都熟悉它，它真的没什么意思。就像，是啊是啊是啊。一旦它用意大利语表演，它就给了我们足够的距离感，让我们能够进入它。你知道，它让我们——就像那个不想要你的情人。你最想要的莫过于那个不想要你的人。对吧？所以这是一样的道理。

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:46.330
<v Susan Vitucci>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Susan Vitucci>[用意大利语演唱]

00:28:46.330 --> 00:29:19.160
<v Jack Hitt>You may recall that when you last heard of Little, back in kindergarten, she was just an average barn door fowl who had an acorn drop on her head, which she mistakenly understood to be the sky falling. Her alarms excited her friends, Goosey Loosey, Turkey Lurkey, and Ducky Lucky. And they join her for a journey to the king to tell him the important news. On the way, they meet up with Sly Fox. Little's pals eagerly accept his invitation for dinner, literally, as it turns out. Fortunately for Little, hunger is not enough to distract her from her mission, and she tracks on.
<v Jack Hitt>你可能记得当你最后一次听到“鸡丁”时，还在幼儿园，她只是一只普通的谷仓禽鸟，头上有个橡子掉下来，她错误地认为那是天要塌了。她的警报惊动了她的朋友，鹅精、火鸡丁和鸭丁。他们加入她，一起去找国王告诉他这个重要消息。路上，他们遇到了狡猾的狐狸。小鸡的朋友们热切地接受了他的邀请去赴宴，正如事实所证明的那样，是字面意思上的邀请。幸运的是，饥饿并不足以分散鸡丁的注意力，让她放弃使命，她继续前行。

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:42.460
<v Jack Hitt>When she meets the king, he tells her that the sky's not falling-- it's just an acorn. So the enlightened Chicken Little returns to her coop, and that's where the story ends. What are we to take away from Little's experience? I like to think it's that Little is rewarded with life, precisely because she went off on this Quixotic mission, totally in the grip of a wrong idea.
<v Jack Hitt>当她见到国王时，他告诉她天并没有塌——那只是个橡子。于是觉悟后的鸡丁回到了她的鸡舍，故事就在那里结束了。我们从鸡丁的经历中能得到什么启示呢？我喜欢这样想，那就是鸡丁因为进行了这次堂吉诃德式的任务，完全沉浸在一个错误的想法中，反而得到了生命的馈赠。

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:56.950
<v Jack Hitt>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Jack Hitt>[用意大利语演唱]

00:29:56.950 --> 00:30:20.980
<v Ira Glass>The children's fable barely figures into the story. It's just one small episode in the life of Chicken Little, now known as La Pulcina Piccola. After the acorn incident, she goes on to become an internationally renowned figure in almost every field imaginable, a diva of politics, academe, theater, art, daring-do. Like Venus, she arrives from some other world, transported on a scallop shell.
<v Ira Glass>这个儿童寓言在故事中几乎没占多少比重。这只是鸡丁（现被称为La Pulcina Piccola）生活中的一个小插曲。橡子事件后，她继续成为几乎所有可想象领域的国际知名人物，政治、学术、戏剧、艺术、冒险的歌剧女主角。就像维纳斯一样，她从另一个世界而来，乘着扇贝壳运送。

00:30:20.980 --> 00:30:39.950
<v Ira Glass>But the triumphs of her life began after a youthful love affair with a fighting cock ends bitterly, and she consoles herself, as we all do at some point in our lives, by plunging into Shakespeare. She becomes an overnight sensation as an actress, celebrated all over the world for one role. Juliet? Cleopatra? Ophelia?
<v Ira Glass>但她人生胜利的开始，是在与一只斗鸡的青春恋情痛苦结束后，她像我们在生命某个阶段所做的那样，通过沉浸在莎士比亚的作品中来安慰自己。她一夜成名，成为了一名女演员，因一个角色而享誉世界。朱丽叶？克利奥帕特拉？奥菲利亚？

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:55.400
<v Susan Vitucci>The company then performs an excerpt of a recreation of her signature role, which is Richard III. Well, you know, I mean, Sarah Bernhardt did Hamlet.
<v Susan Vitucci>剧团随后表演了她标志性角色《理查三世》的片段。嗯，你知道，我是说，莎拉·伯恩哈特还演过哈姆雷特呢。

00:30:55.400 --> 00:31:03.390
<v Jack Hitt>Well, there's a great tradition of women playing the men's roles in Shakespeare, but I think Richard III is one of the more rare roles to be played by a woman.
<v Jack Hitt>嗯，女性在莎士比亚剧中扮演男性角色有着伟大的传统，但我认为《理查三世》是女性扮演的比较罕见的角色之一。

00:31:03.390 --> 00:31:13.160
<v Susan Vitucci>Well, that's how adventuresome an actress this chicken was.
<v Susan Vitucci>好吧，这就是这位鸡演员有多么富有冒险精神。

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:34.130
<v Jack Hitt>I can assure you there's nothing like watching a four inch tall finger puppet crying out, "a horse, a horse-- my kingdom for a horse" in Italian. Not to mention that that puppet is a chicken, surrounded by a whole supporting cast of poultry and other avian supernumeraries. Susan says that, artistically, there's something special about chickens.
<v Jack Hitt>我可以向你保证，没有什么比看着一个四英寸高的手指木偶用意大利语哭喊着：“马，马——我的王国换一匹马”更令人震撼的了。更不用说那个木偶是一只鸡，周围有一群家禽和其他鸟类临时演员支持。苏珊说，在艺术上，鸡有什么特别之处。

00:31:34.130 --> 00:31:45.570
<v Susan Vitucci>They're a clean slate. You can put anything on them. You can project anything onto them. Because it's not like they have, to me, at least, a very strong personality.
<v Susan Vitucci>它们是一张白纸。你可以把任何东西加在它们身上。你可以把任何东西投射到它们身上。因为在我看来，它们不像是有非常强烈个性的那种。

00:31:45.570 --> 00:32:04.710
<v Jack Hitt>Except for La Pulcina. In the opera, she moves into the field of archaeology, masters it, needless to say, and makes a great discovery-- the last tomb of Gallapatra. But not before she sails the seven seas, is ship wrecked, gets rescued-- but it's by pirates-- and then she meets the pirate king.
<v Jack Hitt>除了“小鸡女主角”。在歌剧里，她进入了考古学领域，不用说，精通了这一行，并做出了伟大的发现——加拉帕特拉的最后陵墓。但在那之前，她航行了七大洋，遭遇了海难，被救出——但救她的是海盗——然后她遇到了海盗王。

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:18.580
<v Susan Vitucci>As soon as he meets her, he falls in love with her, because of her sweet spirit. Because she comes in and she says, "here you see a little chicken who, although I'm dripping wet, I'm proud and yellow."
<v Susan Vitucci>他一见到她就爱上了她，因为她高尚的精神。因为她走过来，她说：“在这里你看到一只小鸡，虽然我全身湿透，但我骄傲且是黄色的。”

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:31.330
<v Jack Hitt>Let me repeat that lyric for you in a purer translation. "Although I stand before you, a chicken, who is dripping wet, I am proud and I am yellow." OK. Back to Susan.
<v Jack Hitt>让我为你重复那句歌词的更纯粹的翻译。“虽然我站在你面前，是一只全身湿透的鸡，但我骄傲，我就是黄色的。”好的。回到苏珊的话题。

00:32:31.330 --> 00:32:39.230
<v Susan Vitucci>"And although I've loved and I have lost, I have learned to follow the call of adventure. So let's sail on.
<v Susan Vitucci>“虽然我爱过又失去了，但我学会了跟随冒险的召唤。所以让我们启航吧。”

00:32:39.230 --> 00:32:56.050
<v Susan Vitucci>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Susan Vitucci>[用意大利语演唱]

00:32:56.050 --> 00:33:28.540
<v Jack Hitt>Keep in mind that all of the action-- like everything that occurs in every Susan Vitucci production, ever since the first one for her nephew, and continuing to this day-- occurs among characters created by sticking a small painted Styrofoam ball onto a larger painted Styrofoam ball, poking in two map tacks for eyes, gluing on a tiny felt beak, and then impaling the whole thing on top of one of those really old fashioned clothes pins that a forties cartoon figure would clamp to his nose around a chunk of Limburger cheese.
<v Jack Hitt>请记住，所有的动作——就像苏珊·维图奇每一部作品中发生的一切，自从为她侄子制作的第一部作品以来，直到今天——都发生在使用由一个小彩绘泡沫球粘在一个较大的彩绘泡沫球上，戳进两个地图钉作为眼睛，粘上一个微小的毛毡喙，然后把整个东西插在一根那种40年代卡通人物夹在鼻子上夹臭乳酪的非常老式的衣夹上所创造的角色之间。

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:51.350
<v Jack Hitt>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Jack Hitt>[用意大利语演唱]

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.190
<v Ira Glass>And I could go on.
<v Ira Glass>我还可以继续列举下去。

00:33:53.190 --> 00:34:09.930
<v Ira Glass>Susan has written, or she puts it, "translated" La Pulcina Piccola's diaries, which detail the other adventures that happened in between those in the opera. There are 60 pages so far, excerpts of which have appeared in Clothes Lines, the official fan club newsletter of the opera.
<v Ira Glass>苏珊已经写了，或者用她的话说，“翻译”了La Pulcina Piccola的日记，详细记录了歌剧之外发生的其他冒险。目前已有60页，其中的节选出现在了歌剧官方粉丝俱乐部通讯《衣夹线》上。

00:34:09.930 --> 00:34:36.360
<v Ira Glass>Love's Fowl has a strange effect on people. I didn't understand until Susan loaned me a videotape of one performance. To be honest, I thought I would be annoyed at the intentional irony and hokiness of the puppets. But there I was with my three year old daughter, who loved the show, watching a plastic bird pantomime one of the simplest human moments, but also one of the most profound-- the confession of a great love. In this case, with a cock robin.
<v Ira Glass>《爱的家禽》对人们有种奇特的影响。在苏珊借给我一份表演录像带之前我并不理解。老实说，我原以为会因为木偶的刻意讽刺和老套而感到恼火。但当我带着我三岁的女儿一起看时，她喜欢这个节目，看着一只塑料鸟模仿人类最简单的时刻，也是最深刻的时刻之一——承认伟大的爱情。在这种情况下，是与一只知更鸟。

00:34:36.360 --> 00:35:02.230
<v Susan Vitucci>The song that she sings as she enters goes, "I am a chicken and ready for love. My heart is as fragile as the egg from which I was born. Treat me gently, and so will I treat you. Together from earthly love, we will reach for the divine." And then she sings, "I am a chicken, and I can't fly without love. My heart, it is as strong as the egg from which I was born." And so forth.
<v Susan Vitucci>她出场时唱的歌词是：“我是一只鸡，准备好去爱。我的心就像我出生的蛋一样脆弱。温柔地对待我，我也将那样对待你。让我们一起从世俗的爱中，去触摸神圣。”然后她唱道：“我是一只鸡，没有爱我就无法飞翔。我的心，它像我出生的蛋一样坚强。”等等。

00:35:02.230 --> 00:35:07.100
<v Susan Vitucci>And so it is only with Cock Robin that she flies.
<v Susan Vitucci>所以只有和知更鸟在一起，她才能飞翔。

00:35:07.100 --> 00:35:31.360
<v Susan Vitucci>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Susan Vitucci>[用意大利语演唱]

00:35:31.360 --> 00:35:58.890
<v Jack Hitt>And after they've agreed to fly together, and they're soaring in the air, Cock Robin is shot and killed, murdered by a jealous sparrow. I couldn't believe it, but I was getting choked up, especially when Cock Robin appeared on the stage, his Styrofoam body spray painted black for the lament, has little magic marker eyes drawn as x's. I gathered my daughter in my arms and held on tight, as I was helplessly drawn into an expression of the grief and suffering of this little sad bird.
<v Jack Hitt>当他们同意一起飞翔，翱翔在空中时，知更鸟被一颗嫉妒的麻雀射杀并杀死了。我简直不敢相信，但我哽咽了，特别是当知更鸟出现在舞台上时，它那泡沫塑料做的身体被喷成了黑色以表达哀悼，还有用马克笔画的小叉作为眼睛。我把女儿搂在怀里紧紧抱住，我无助地被拉进了这种对这只悲伤的小鸟的悲痛和苦难的表达中。

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:15.050
<v Jack Hitt>In this era of slick special effects, there was something unexpectedly liberating in the marriage of this crude medium-- painted Styrofoam balls bobbing up and down behind a cardboard box-- and the high melodramatic art of Italian opera. Picture it.
<v Jack Hitt>在这个特效花哨的时代，这种粗糙媒介的联姻——纸箱后面上下起伏的彩绘泡沫球——与意大利歌剧高亢的情节艺术相结合，有着意想不到的解放感。想象一下。

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:48.450
<v Jack Hitt>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Jack Hitt>[用意大利语演唱]

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:51.140
<v Ira Glass>I want a subscription to that newsletter.
<v Ira Glass>我想订阅那个时事通讯。

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:57.150
<v Jack Hitt>Are you going to do this? I mean, are you going to be working with Pulcina Piccola, you think, for the rest of your life?
<v Jack Hitt>你要一直做这个吗？我是说，你觉得你会为了你的余生都致力于Pulcina Piccola的工作吗？

00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:10.910
<v Susan Vitucci>It's possible. And I like working with her because I get to go into a world that's inhabited by a very sweet spirit. And play with the mechanics of the world.
<v Susan Vitucci>有可能。我喜欢和她一起工作，因为我能进入一个居住着非常甜美精神的世界。并在这个世界的机制中玩耍。

00:37:10.910 --> 00:37:46.580
<v Susan Vitucci>And because it's very small-- I could never have afforded to produce this show with people. But I could afford to do it with clothespins. So I can do as big a production as I want with clothespins. I can have stuff fly in and out and come in from traps, and I can have all kinds of fancy, flashy stuff that costs millions of dollars to do on Broadway. And it costs me $200 because I had to buy lots and lots of Styrofoam and clothespins and stuff, and all this, and a new table maybe. And I get to do whatever I want.
<v Susan Vitucci>而且因为它是非常小的——我永远负担不起用真人制作这个节目。但我负担得起用衣夹来做。所以我可以随心所欲地制作像百老汇那样大的制作。我可以让东西飞进飞出，从活板门进来，我可以有各种各样花哨的、闪耀的东西，在百老汇做这些要花几百万美元。而对我来说只花200美元，因为我得买大量的泡沫塑料、衣夹之类的东西，还有一张新桌子也许。我可以随心所欲做任何我想做的事。

00:37:46.580 --> 00:37:58.160
<v Susan Vitucci>[SINGING IN ITALIAN]
<v Susan Vitucci>[用意大利语演唱]

00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:02.400
<v Ira Glass>Jack Hitt is a writer who lives in New Haven.
<v Ira Glass>杰克·希特是一位居住在纽黑文的作家。

00:38:02.400 --> 00:38:18.240
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "FINGER LICKIN' GOOD" BY THE BEASTIE BOYS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - 野兽男孩的《手指舔得好》]

00:38:18.240 --> 00:38:21.680
<v Ira Glass>Act Four-- Trying To Respect The Chicken.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕——试图尊重鸡。

00:38:21.680 --> 00:38:31.430
<v Ira Glass>Sure, it's one thing to take a fictional character like Chicken Little and make her into a star. Try doing that with a real chicken. Just try.
<v Ira Glass>当然，拿“鸡丁”这样的虚构角色并让她成为明星是一回事。尝试用一只真正的鸡来做这件事。试试看。

00:38:31.430 --> 00:38:48.930
<v Tamara Staples>Well, these are photographs of chickens. The first one here is a Silver Laced Wyandotte. It's a black and white bird, essentially, but the tail feathers have a lot of iridescent green coloring.
<v Tamara Staples>嗯，这些是鸡的照片。第一张是一只银纹怀恩多特鸡。这基本上是一只黑白相间的鸟，但尾羽有很多绚丽的绿色色彩。

00:38:48.930 --> 00:39:01.420
<v Ira Glass>In a world where chickens get no respect, Tamara Staples treats them the way the humans treat those we revere most. She takes their portraits lovingly. Her shots are like fashion photographs, beautifully-lit, color backdrops. They're beautiful.
<v Ira Glass>在这个鸡得不到尊重的世界里，塔玛拉·斯台普斯用我们崇敬人类的方式对待它们。她充满爱意地拍摄它们的肖像。她的拍摄就像时装摄影，有精美的照明、彩色背景。它们很漂亮。

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:08.000
<v Ira Glass>The first one looked regal, but now you've just turned to one where it almost looks like-- it's like a clown. it looks comic.
<v Ira Glass>第一张看起来很威严，但你刚才转到了一张几乎看起来像——就像个小丑。看起来很滑稽。

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:15.530
<v Tamara Staples>Mm-hm. [AFFIRMATIVE] It's a modeled Houdan, which I always sort of call the Phyllis Diller chicken, which is--
<v Tamara Staples>嗯。[肯定] 这是一个斑点乌丹鸡，我总是把它称为菲利斯·狄勒（喜剧演员）鸡，也就是——

00:39:15.530 --> 00:39:18.560
<v Ira Glass>Oh, my God. That chicken does look like Phyllis Diller.
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪。那只鸡看起来真的像菲利斯·狄勒。

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:27.530
<v Tamara Staples>It does. It's the hat. It looks like it's got this huge feathered hat sort of thing, and a strange body shape.
<v Tamara Staples>确实。就是那顶帽子。它看起来好像戴着一顶巨大的羽毛帽子之类东西，还有奇怪的身形。

00:39:27.530 --> 00:39:41.800
<v Ira Glass>In a way, it's like Tamara Staples is running an odd little cross-species science experiment, one that asks this question: what happens when you try to treat a chicken the way we treat humans? Even if it's just for the length of a photo shoot.
<v Ira Glass>某种程度上，塔玛拉·斯台普斯就像是在进行一个奇怪的跨物种科学实验，一个提出了这样一个问题：当你试图以对待人类的方式对待鸡时会发生什么？即使只是在拍摄的一段时间里。

00:39:41.800 --> 00:40:01.430
<v Ira Glass>What happens, it turns out, is that you learn just where the thin line is that divides human beings from birds. All right. Maybe it's not such a thin line, but it's definitely a line, and, like most city people, I had never thought about it. About where it lays, about what it might be, what it might consist of, until Tamara and I headed out to a farm.
<v Ira Glass>结果证明发生的事情是，你了解了区分人类和鸟类的界限有多薄。好了。也许这不是那么薄的界限，但它绝对是一个界限，像大多数城市人一样，我从来没想过它。关于它在哪，它可能是什么，它可能由什么组成，直到塔玛拉和我出发前往一个农场。

00:40:01.430 --> 00:40:04.380
<v Ira Glass>[ROOSTER CROWING]
<v Ira Glass>[公鸡打鸣声]

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:09.780
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKENS CLUCKING]
<v Ira Glass>[鸡咯咯声]

00:40:09.780 --> 00:40:11.580
<v Paul Davidson>I think that is the best one.
<v Paul Davidson>我想那只是最好的一只。

00:40:11.580 --> 00:40:15.300
<v Tamara Staples>Yeah, we've got to get him. We don't want him to get dirty or anything, do we? Or does it matter?
<v Tamara Staples>是的，我们必须抓住它。我们不想让它变脏什么的，对吧？或者没关系？

00:40:15.300 --> 00:40:16.590
<v Paul Davidson>She runs loose every day.
<v Paul Davidson>她每天都在外面散步。

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:17.540
<v Tamara Staples>Can you find her?
<v Tamara Staples>你能找到她吗？

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:19.050
<v Paul Davidson>Yeah, we can take her out.
<v Paul Davidson>是的，我们可以把她带出来。

00:40:19.050 --> 00:40:22.630
<v Tamara Staples>We're going to get him to-- we're going to have to wrangle them, you know.
<v Tamara Staples>我们要抓住它们，你知道。

00:40:22.630 --> 00:40:46.940
<v Ira Glass>We're at the Davidson's Dairy Farm, about an hour and a half northwest of Chicago. Family members present-- Paul, who's helping Tamara choose a bird to photograph, his sister Laura, who's studying photography at a nearby university, the grandfather, George Cairns, a veteran breeder, their father, Dick, who seems the most skeptical of this whole project, but who patiently shows Tamara and her assistant, Dennis, the milking barn as a possible place to set up and shoot.
<v Ira Glass>我们在戴维森奶牛场，距离芝加哥西北部约一个半小时路程。在场的家庭成员——保罗，他正在帮塔玛拉挑选拍摄的鸟，他的妹妹劳拉，她在附近的一所大学学习摄影，爷爷乔治·凯恩斯，一位资深饲养员，他们的父亲迪克，他看起来对整个项目最怀疑，但他耐心地带塔玛拉和她的助手丹尼斯去挤奶棚作为设置和拍摄的可能场所。

00:40:46.940 --> 00:40:49.860
<v Dick Davidson>What kind of an area are you looking for?
<v Dick Davidson>你们在寻找什么样的区域？

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:58.800
<v Tamara Staples>Well, maybe-- it could be a little wider, don't you think? And if it could be from here to there, and know that pole to that pole.
<v Tamara Staples>嗯，也许——它可能再宽一点，你不觉得吗？如果是从这里到那里，知道那根杆子到那根杆子。

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:00.400
<v Dick Davidson>For what?
<v Dick Davidson>为了什么？

00:41:00.400 --> 00:41:01.600
<v Dick Davidson>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Dick Davidson>[说话声重叠]

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:05.650
<v Tamara Staples>Well, we are set-- maybe this is a good time to pull out the portfolio.
<v Tamara Staples>嗯，我们设置好了——也许现在是拿出作品集的好时机。

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:06.540
<v Assistant Dennis>OK.
<v Assistant Dennis>好的。

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:08.770
<v Tamara Staples>You want to grab it?
<v Tamara Staples>你想抓起它吗？

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:20.880
<v Tamara Staples>I'm actually-- it's a study of the birds. But it's an isolated study, so it doesn't-- people aren't necessarily associating it with the farm and something to eat.
<v Tamara Staples>我实际上是——这是一个关于鸟类的研究。但这是一个孤立的研究，所以它不——人们不一定把它与农场和可以吃的东西联系起来。

00:41:20.880 --> 00:41:37.530
<v Ira Glass>Tamara takes us all outside the barn-- so dust won't get on her photos-- and shows them her shots, name dropping the names of some big chicken people, people whose birds she's photographed. Including Bob Wulff, editor of The Poultry Press. Dick notices that a bird in one photo has crooked toes.
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉带我们所有人走出谷仓——这样灰尘就不会弄到她的照片上——并向他们展示了她的拍摄作品，还顺便提到了她拍摄过的一些“大鸡人物”，比如《家禽新闻》的编辑鲍勃·沃尔夫。迪克注意到一张照片里的一只鸟有弯曲的脚趾。

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:43.450
<v Dick Davidson>Probably on a hard surface you turn--
<v Dick Davidson>可能是在坚硬的表面上你转——

00:41:43.450 --> 00:41:46.860
<v Ira Glass>What do you guys think of the pictures?
<v Ira Glass>你们觉得这些照片怎么样？

00:41:46.860 --> 00:41:58.720
<v Dick Davidson>Well, the pictures are nice and sharp. I mean, there's nothing wrong with the pictures. If there's anything to find fault with, it's the birds. You know, they aren't posing the way they should, some of them.
<v Dick Davidson>嗯，这些照片很漂亮，很清晰。我是说，照片本身没什么问题。如果有什么可以挑剔的，那就是那些鸟。你知道，它们没有按照它们应该的方式摆姿势，有些是这样。

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:23.360
<v Ira Glass>Fact is, while city people usually go nuts when they see Tamara's pictures, a lot of chicken breeders don't like them. And to understand why, to fully comprehend this little culture clash here in America, we have to leave the barnyard for a minute and flash back to something that happened back at Tamara's apartment in the city.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，虽然城市人在看到塔玛拉的照片时通常会发疯，但许多养鸡人不喜欢它们。要理解原因，要完全理解这里在美国发生的这种小文化冲突，我们必须离开谷仓一分钟，回闪到塔玛拉在城市公寓里发生的事情。

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:33.130
<v Ira Glass>Tamara showed me this old red book from the turn of the century. This is a book the seal of the American Poultry Association in gold on the front. And then, right there in gold letters--
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉给我看了这本世纪之交的老红书。这是一本封面上有美国家禽协会金印的书。然后，就在那儿，用金色的字母写着——

00:42:33.130 --> 00:42:42.710
<v Tamara Staples>Standard of Perfection. The Standard of Perfection is really the bible of poultry standards. You know, what birds are--
<v Tamara Staples>《完美标准》。《完美标准》确实是家禽标准的圣经。你知道，鸟是什么——

00:42:42.710 --> 00:42:57.580
<v Ira Glass>Tamara flipped past the engravings and illustrations of chickens of all types and breeds. These were show chickens, standing the way that chickens stand in competitions. Then Tamara pulled out one of her own photos to compare, to show me how her poses do not meet the standard in the book.
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉翻过了各种类型和品种的鸡的雕刻和插图。这些是展示鸡，按照鸡在比赛中站立的方式站立。然后塔玛拉拿出她自己的一张照片进行比较，向我展示她的摆姿势是如何不符合书中的标准。

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:14.810
<v Tamara Staples>The tail needs to be higher. Her feet are not erect, standing. Chest isn't out. Head, it needs to be up more. And it shows-- I mean, you can see the shape of the chicken much better in the Standard of Perfection pose.
<v Tamara Staples>尾巴需要更高。她的脚不是挺直地站着。胸部没有挺出来。头，需要再抬高一些。它显示——我是说，在《完美标准》的姿势中，你可以看得更清楚鸡的形状。

00:43:14.810 --> 00:43:19.880
<v Ira Glass>See, to me, what's so interesting, though, is that the Standard of Perfection doesn't include a personality.
<v Ira Glass>看，对我来说，最有趣的是《完美标准》不包括个性。

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:25.190
<v Tamara Staples>Right. Because it's not about personality. It's about breeding.
<v Tamara Staples>对。因为它不是关于个性的。它是关于繁育的。

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:31.510
<v Ira Glass>And so is that a pose that the owners would want to own a photo of?
<v Ira Glass>那么这是一种主人想要拥有照片的姿势吗？

00:43:31.510 --> 00:43:44.160
<v Tamara Staples>They are very particular about this-- they want to see their bird in the Standard of Perfection pose. Definitely. Because that's what they've been taught from 4-H, when they were kids, to do.
<v Tamara Staples>他们对这个非常讲究——他们想看到他们的鸟以《完美标准》的姿势展示。绝对的。因为那是他们从小在4-H俱乐部里被教导要做的。

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:51.840
<v Ira Glass>That's for them. For herself, for her city customers, she uses the others. OK, back to the barnyard.
<v Ira Glass>那是给他们看的。对她自己，对她的城市顾客，她用的是其他的。好了，回到谷仓。

00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:56.540
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKEN SQUAKING]
<v Ira Glass>[鸡尖叫声]

00:43:56.540 --> 00:44:15.860
<v Ira Glass>Tamara and the Davidsons decide to set up the photo session in a room that's usually used to store feed for the cows. It takes about 45 minutes to set this up. That 45 minutes includes dismantling and moving a wall of hay that is probably 10 feet high and 15 feet long. This takes five people.
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉和戴维森一家决定在一个通常用来储存牛饲料的房间里设置拍摄环节。设置这个花了大约45分钟。那45分钟包括拆除和移动一面可能是10英尺高、15英尺长的干草墙。这花了五个人。

00:44:15.860 --> 00:44:25.150
<v Ira Glass>Then in comes the power and the fancy lights and the cloth backdrop that gets hung from the steel pole. The backdrop is ironed first with an iron and ironing board brought from the city just for that purpose.
<v Ira Glass>然后是电力和花哨的灯光，以及挂在钢管上的布背景。背景首先用从城市专门带来的熨斗和熨衣板熨平。

00:44:25.150 --> 00:44:28.330
<v Tamara Staples>11 and 1/2, 11, and an 8 and 1/2?
<v Tamara Staples>11.5，11，还有一个8.5？

00:44:28.330 --> 00:44:33.900
<v Assistant Dennis>Yeah. 11 and 1/2-- your test is going to be at 11 and 1/2, 11, and 8 and 1/2. We're going to shoot your film at 11.
<v Assistant Dennis>是的。11.5——你的测试将在11.5、11和8.5。我们要用11的光圈拍你的胶片。

00:44:33.900 --> 00:44:40.870
<v Ira Glass>It was cold. Well below freezing. So cold that the Polaroid film that Tamara uses for lighting tests would not fully develop.
<v Ira Glass>天很冷。远低于冰点。冷到塔玛拉用来测试灯光的宝丽来胶片无法完全显影。

00:44:40.870 --> 00:44:42.170
<v Paul Davidson>You ready for the bird?
<v Paul Davidson>准备好那只鸟了吗？

00:44:42.170 --> 00:44:46.350
<v Tamara Staples>We're close. Just want to commune with the bird.
<v Tamara Staples>快了。只想和那只鸟交流一下。

00:44:46.350 --> 00:44:55.870
<v Tamara Staples>We just want to make you pretty. Look how sweet, aren't you? You know what, I'm going to photograph you. My name is Tamara, I'll be your photographer for today.
<v Tamara Staples>我们只想让你变漂亮。看多甜美，你不是吗？你知道吗，我要拍你。我叫塔玛拉，我将是你今天的摄影师。

00:44:55.870 --> 00:45:06.900
<v Ira Glass>Our first bird is a white Cornish, a show bird who belongs to George. His show bird is used to being picked up and handled. Part of preparing chickens for shows involves handling them a lot so they'll be calm with the judges.
<v Ira Glass>我们的第一只鸟是一只白科尼什鸡，一只属于乔治的展示鸟。他的展示鸟习惯了被捡起和处理。为展示准备鸡的一部分工作涉及大量的处理，这样它们在评委面前才会冷静。

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:18.610
<v Tamara Staples>If you could just nudge his head up a little bit, he's perfect. He's got his chest out. Now he's got his face in-- OK. Yeah, you know what we want. Yeah, you're-- great, George. He's got a feather on his back here.
<v Tamara Staples>如果你能稍微把它的头抬起来一点，它就很完美了。它的胸部挺出来了。现在它的脸进去了——好的。是的，你知道我们想要什么。是的，你——太棒了，乔治。它的背上有根羽毛。

00:45:18.610 --> 00:45:23.360
<v Ira Glass>Tamara has the Cornish stand up on a stack of little red antique books-- kind of unsteady.
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉让科尼什鸡站在一堆小红色古董书上——有点摇晃。

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:40.320
<v Ira Glass>Things go well for a while. She gets a half dozen good shots of the bird. Expressive shots. More personality than Standard of Perfection, George tells me. The bird's chest isn't high enough, it's body is not turned correctly to the camera. And then the bird stops cooperating. He gets tired. Paul has a suggestion.
<v Ira Glass>事情顺利了一会儿。她拍了半打那只鸟的好照片。富有表现力的照片。乔治告诉我，比《完美标准》有更多的个性。这只鸟的胸部不够高，它的身体没有正确地转向相机。然后那只鸟停止了合作。它累了。保罗有一个建议。

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:41.980
<v Paul Davidson>Bring in a pull-it.
<v Paul Davidson>带来一只母鸡。

00:45:41.980 --> 00:45:44.870
<v Tamara Staples>You know what? You know that works.
<v Tamara Staples>你知道吗？那管用。

00:45:44.870 --> 00:45:49.490
<v Ira Glass>Maybe we should explain what that is. What does that mean to bring a pull-it?
<v Ira Glass>也许我们应该解释一下那是什么。带来一只母鸡意味着什么？

00:45:49.490 --> 00:45:55.340
<v George Cairns>Thinks maybe a female will perk him up.
<v George Cairns>觉得也许雌性会使它振作起来。

00:45:55.340 --> 00:46:02.540
<v Ira Glass>Laura grabs a hen and waves it at the flaccid cock. The cock does not rise. I can say that on the radio, right?
<v Ira Glass>劳拉抓住一只母鸡，对着那只没精打采的公鸡挥舞。公鸡没有站起来。我在广播里可以这么说吧？

00:46:02.540 --> 00:46:07.230
<v Paul Davidson>Well, it probably would have been better to get the one from the other pen that he's not used to.
<v Paul Davidson>嗯，可能最好是从它不熟悉的另一个鸡舍里抓一只。

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:12.890
<v Tamara Staples>Fresh blood. Bring him around a little bit so his back--
<v Tamara Staples>新鲜血液。带它转一转，让它的背——

00:46:12.890 --> 00:46:18.540
<v Ira Glass>For real? The chicken-- the rooster will show off more for a hen that it doesn't know?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？鸡——公鸡会在它不认识的母鸡面前展示更多吗？

00:46:18.540 --> 00:46:27.100
<v Paul Davidson>Yes. If you put a new hen in with him, or him in with a group a new hens, he will really show off.
<v Paul Davidson>是的。如果你把一只新母鸡放进它那里，或者把它放进一群新母鸡中，它会真的展示一番。

00:46:27.100 --> 00:46:35.000
<v Ira Glass>They try this and that, nothing with much success. Finally, with one shot left, Paul suggests putting a hen into the picture with the rooster.
<v Ira Glass>他们试了这个和那个，没什么太大的成功。最后，剩下最后一张照片时，保罗建议在照片中放一只母鸡和公鸡。

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:40.160
<v Tamara Staples>Get the girl to-- she looks like her feet are so far apart, she's really struggling to stand.
<v Tamara Staples>让那个女孩——她看起来脚分得那么开，她在努力地站着。

00:46:40.160 --> 00:46:41.660
<v Paul Davidson>That's the way they stand, though.
<v Paul Davidson>那是它们站立的方式。

00:46:41.660 --> 00:46:42.650
<v Dick Davidson>[INAUDIBLE PHRASE].
<v Dick Davidson>[听不清]。

00:46:42.650 --> 00:46:46.370
<v Tamara Staples>That's all right, that's all right. Ah-- [CAMERA CLICKS] Oh, did you see that?
<v Tamara Staples>没关系，没关系。啊——[相机快门声] 哦，你看到了吗？

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:46.880
<v Laura Davidson>Yeah.
<v Laura Davidson>是的。

00:46:46.880 --> 00:46:47.750
<v Tamara Staples>All right, we got it.
<v Tamara Staples>好的，我们拍到了。

00:46:47.750 --> 00:46:48.230
<v Ira Glass>Why? What did she just do? Describe--
<v Ira Glass>为什么？她刚才做了什么？描述一下——

00:46:48.230 --> 00:47:02.300
<v Tamara Staples>She looked up at him very sweetly. Like that. With her head cocked. The male bird was posing, and she was posing also, but had a personality of just being like the sweet, doting mother, you now?
<v Tamara Staples>她很甜美地看着它。就像那样。歪着头。那只公鸟在摆姿势，她也在摆姿势，但有一种就像甜美、慈爱的母亲那样的个性，你懂吗？

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:04.900
<v Ira Glass>But not Standard of Perfection.
<v Ira Glass>但不符合《完美标准》。

00:47:04.900 --> 00:47:08.420
<v Tamara Staples>But not Standard of Perfection.
<v Tamara Staples>但不符合《完美标准》。

00:47:08.420 --> 00:47:11.800
<v Tamara Staples>So we're done with this background, and--
<v Tamara Staples>所以我们这个背景拍完了，然后——

00:47:11.800 --> 00:47:58.070
<v Ira Glass>Not Standard of Perfection. Even these perfectly bred Cornishes could not achieve the Standard of Perfection today. And even in this goofy, un-bird-like situation, an hour of watching them makes clear just how hard it is to ever get birds to hit the standard. Which is to say not only do we completely dominate every aspect of the lives of chickens-- their births, their feed, their eggs, their slaughter-- not only have we bred them to human specifications to meet human needs, but we have created a standard for what it means to be a chicken that most chickens can never meet. That's what the standard means. We judge them as chickens, and we find them lacking. If they had brains to understand this, they would be right to feel indignant.
<v Ira Glass>不符合《完美标准》。即使是这些完美培育的科尼什鸡今天也无法达到《完美标准》。即使在这种笨拙的、不像鸟的情况中，一个小时观察它们让人们清楚地看到让鸟类达到标准有多困难。也就是说，我们不仅完全主导了鸡生活的方方面面——它们的出生、饲料、鸡蛋、屠宰——我们不仅为了满足人类的需求而按人类规范培育它们，而且我们创造了一个关于鸡意味着什么的“标准”，大多数鸡永远无法达到。这就是标准的意思。我们评价它们是鸡，却发现它们不合格。如果它们有大脑能理解这一点，它们是有理由感到愤怒的。

00:47:58.070 --> 00:48:20.560
<v Ira Glass>But of course, this is a city person's perspective, and that means it is completely wrong-headed from the point of view of anybody who actually raises birds. Standing in the cold feed room, I had a long, long talk with George about this. George is 80 years old. He's been raising birds since the-- I guess the Calvin Coolidge administration. And he says the whole fun of raising birds is raising them to the standard.
<v Ira Glass>但当然，这是城市人的视角，这意味着从任何真正养鸟的人的角度来看，这是完全错误的。站在寒冷的饲料室里，我就此和乔治进行了长时间的交谈。乔治80岁了。他自——我想是卡尔文·柯立芝政府时期就开始养鸟了。他说养鸟的全部乐趣在于把它们养成标准。

00:48:20.560 --> 00:48:52.340
<v George Cairns>Well, like, for instance, if your birds lack bone, OK, you go out and buy a bird as near to like them as you can with better bone. But when you mate them together, you might get long-legged birds, or too short. I mean, you don't get what you want just by mating. It takes four or five years to gradually get it out, and by that time, they're inbred and you need new ones.
<v George Cairns>嗯，比如，如果你的鸟缺乏骨骼，好吧，你就去买一只尽可能像它们但骨骼更好的鸟。但当你把它们配对在一起时，你可能会得到长腿的鸟，或者太短的。我是说，你不能仅仅通过配对得到你想要的。这需要四五年时间慢慢把它弄出来，到那时，它们已经近亲繁殖，你需要新的。

00:48:52.340 --> 00:49:01.670
<v Ira Glass>George tells me that when he's breeding a new batch of birds, he'll hatch 65 of them, and only one or two will be anywhere near the Standard of Perfection. That's how hard it is.
<v Ira Glass>乔治告诉我，当他繁育一批新鸟时，他会孵化65只，只有一两只会接近《完美标准》。这就是它有多难。

00:49:01.670 --> 00:49:04.640
<v Ira Glass>Do you get frustrated with the Standard Of Protection sometimes?
<v Ira Glass>你有时会对《保护标准》感到沮丧吗？

00:49:04.640 --> 00:49:12.120
<v George Cairns>No, we get frustrated with the judges. Because every judge has his own idea of what the standard should be.
<v George Cairns>不，我们对评委感到沮丧。因为每个评委都有自己关于标准应该是什么样的想法。

00:49:12.120 --> 00:49:14.190
<v Ira Glass>I thought that's the whole point of a standard, is that--
<v Ira Glass>我以为这就是标准的全部意义所在，那就是——

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:33.000
<v George Cairns>That is. But one judge will want it this way, and another another. Today, if you bred your birds to the standard of perfection-- weight and everything-- and took them to the show, you probably wouldn't get anywhere. You've got to breed to the fads.
<v George Cairns>确实。但一个评委想要这样，另一个想要那样。今天，如果你按照完美标准——重量等等——繁育你的鸟，并把它们带去展示，你可能什么也得不到。你必须按潮流繁育。

00:49:33.000 --> 00:49:52.700
<v Ira Glass>That's right. The fads. Like Cornishes these days are supposed to have shorter legs than the real Standard of Perfection. Vertical tail feathers are out in all sorts of breeds that really should have them. In the country, among the chicken breeders, they think about a lot of things you never get to in the city.
<v Ira Glass>没错。潮流。比如科尼什鸡这些日子应该比真正的《完美标准》腿更短。垂直的尾羽在很多本该有的品种中已经过时了。在乡村，在养鸡人中间，他们考虑了很多你在城市里永远不会触及的事情。

00:49:52.700 --> 00:50:01.880
<v Ira Glass>And when you're raising these birds, with any of these birds, do you have a close relationship with a bird the way some do [UNINTELLIGIBLE] with a pet?
<v Ira Glass>当你养这些鸟时，对于这些鸟中的任何一只，你有像有些人对待宠物那样和某只鸟有亲密关系吗？

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:26.320
<v George Cairns>I don't have time. Yeah, I got too many things to do. See, a few years ago, I almost died of cancer, and the Good Lord told me how to cure myself. And so I've been working with that a lot the last three years. I've been helping people, and put in papers. Now it's getting all over the United States.
<v George Cairns>我没时间。是的，我有太多事要做了。看，几年前，我差点死于癌症，上帝告诉我如何治愈自己。所以我过去三年一直在那方面做很多工作。我一直在帮助人们，并提交论文。现在它正在遍布美国。

00:50:26.320 --> 00:50:27.890
<v Ira Glass>What did you do? What do you do?
<v Ira Glass>你做了什么？你做什么？

00:50:27.890 --> 00:50:38.100
<v George Cairns>It's you use the root of a dandelion. Simple as can be. But there's something in that that builds up your blood and your immune system.
<v George Cairns>它是使用蒲公英的根。简单得不能再简单了。但其中有什么东西能增强你的血液和免疫系统。

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:43.500
<v Ira Glass>Wait a second. You're saying that you were diagnosed with cancer, and this is the only treatment you've had, and it cured you?
<v Ira Glass>等一下。你是说你被诊断出患有癌症，而这是你接受过的唯一治疗，它治愈了你？

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:55.010
<v George Cairns>Yeah. And I've given it to other people when the medical world has told them that there's nothing more they can do, and they've gotten well, too. But not all of them. If they're too far gone, it won't help them.
<v George Cairns>是的。当医疗界告诉他们已经无能为力时，我也给了别人，他们也好了。但并不是所有的人。如果他们病得太重，它帮不了他们。

00:50:55.010 --> 00:50:58.540
<v Ira Glass>And you make it into tea or something like that?
<v Ira Glass>你把它做成茶之类的东西吗？

00:50:58.540 --> 00:51:06.840
<v George Cairns>We just put it in a little water, a little milk, Kool-Aide. You can put it on a sandwich. Anything that isn't hot.
<v George Cairns>我们只是把它放在一点水、一点牛奶、酷艾乐饮料里。你可以把它放在三明治上。任何不热的东西。

00:51:06.840 --> 00:51:23.200
<v Ira Glass>George gives me a pamphlet that he's written up. No doctor has actually checked him out to prove the cancer is gone from his body. He's actually got no hard scientific proof that this really works, but he says God told him that this is the way he should be spending his time. And it has cut into his bird breeding a bit.
<v Ira Glass>乔治给了我一本他写的小册子。没有医生真正检查过他以证明他体内的癌症已经消失。他实际上没有任何确凿的科学证据证明这真的有效，但他说上帝告诉他这是他应该花时间做的方式。它确实让他养鸟的工作减少了一些。

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:35.620
<v Ira Glass>George leaves, off on other business. Tamara is finished hanging and lighting the next backdrop. And the rest of us begin with the second bird, a bird called a Brahma, with elaborately patterned brown and white feathers.
<v Ira Glass>乔治离开了，去处理其他事务。塔玛拉完成了挂起和照明下一个背景。我们其余的人开始处理第二只鸟，一种叫婆罗门鸡的鸟，有着精心装饰的棕色和白色羽毛。

00:51:35.620 --> 00:51:37.070
<v Ira Glass>[BIRD CLUCKING]
<v Ira Glass>[鸟的咯咯声]

00:51:37.070 --> 00:51:39.470
<v Paul Davidson>Got her.
<v Paul Davidson>抓到她了。

00:51:39.470 --> 00:51:44.480
<v Ira Glass>She is big. This is a chicken like the size of a dog.
<v Ira Glass>她很大。这是一只像狗那么大的鸡。

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:45.680
<v Paul Davidson>Not that big.
<v Paul Davidson>没那么大。

00:51:45.680 --> 00:51:46.750
<v Ira Glass>A small dog.
<v Ira Glass>一只小狗。

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:48.690
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHING]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:51:48.690 --> 00:52:06.150
<v Ira Glass>Our second bird demonstrates the great distance between bird, instinct, and intelligence. And the demands of modern fashion photography, which is to say, of civilization. Called upon to do human tasks, even rather passive ones, a bird remains a bird.
<v Ira Glass>我们的第二只鸟展示了鸟类、本能和智慧之间的巨大鸿沟。以及现代时尚摄影的需求，也就是说，现代文明的需求。被要求做人类的任务，甚至是相当被动的任务，鸟终究还是鸟。

00:52:06.150 --> 00:52:09.130
<v Ira Glass>Paul carries the huge hen onto the fragile little set Tamara has built.
<v Ira Glass>保罗把那只巨大的母鸡扛到塔玛拉建造的脆弱小布景上。

00:52:09.130 --> 00:52:11.840
<v Tamara Staples>He's a beauty. What ya eating there, buddy?
<v Tamara Staples>她是位美女。你在吃什么，伙计？

00:52:11.840 --> 00:52:13.090
<v Tamara Staples>[BIRD SQUAKING]
<v Tamara Staples>[鸟的尖叫声]

00:52:13.090 --> 00:52:16.520
<v Tamara Staples>Whew, it slapped me.
<v Tamara Staples>呼，它拍了我一下。

00:52:16.520 --> 00:52:31.430
<v Ira Glass>"I'm scared of this one," she says, quietly, as she adjusts her camera. The chicken is so big-- nine pounds, the size of a small consumer turkey-- that she has to pull the camera back. the Davidsons are looking at her skeptically. Paul asked pointedly if she's ever shot a bird this big.
<v Ira Glass>“我害怕这个，”她轻声说道，同时调整相机。这只鸡太大了——九磅重，一只小型的消费火鸡那么大——她不得不把相机往后拉。戴维森一家怀疑地看着她。保罗直截了当地问她是否拍过这么大的鸟。

00:52:31.430 --> 00:52:33.280
<v Tamara Staples>We've got to figure out where the--
<v Tamara Staples>我们得弄清楚哪——

00:52:33.280 --> 00:52:33.750
<v Tamara Staples>[BIRD SQUACKING]
<v Tamara Staples>[鸟的尖叫声]

00:52:33.750 --> 00:52:35.160
<v Dick Davidson>Whoa.
<v Dick Davidson>哇。

00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:36.570
<v Dick Davidson>[BIRD CLUCKING]
<v Dick Davidson>[鸟的咯咯声]

00:52:36.570 --> 00:52:39.010
<v Tamara Staples>Hello, bird. Are you going to slap me in the face again? I hope not.
<v Tamara Staples>你好，鸟儿。你还要再拍我的脸吗？我希望不要。

00:52:39.010 --> 00:52:41.740
<v Paul Davidson>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] jump right in your face.
<v Paul Davidson>[听不清] 直接跳到你的脸上。

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:54.540
<v Tamara Staples>You know why you're here? Let's talk. We need you to be beautiful. Here's your moment. OK? There are more where you came from, buddy. You better act up here.
<v Tamara Staples>你知道你为什么在这儿吗？我们谈谈。我们需要你变得漂亮。这是你的时刻。好吗？你来的地方还有很多，伙计。你最好在这儿表现好点。

00:52:54.540 --> 00:53:04.620
<v Ira Glass>This combination of coddling and threats might motivate an aspiring supermodel, or an eager puppy. But this, after all, is a chicken. Laura tries to lure it up with a handful of corn.
<v Ira Glass>这种溺爱和威胁的结合可能会激励一个有抱负的超级名模，或者一只急切的小狗。但这终究是一只鸡。劳拉试着用一把玉米把它诱引上来。

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:05.160
<v Laura Davidson>She standing--
<v Laura Davidson>她站着——

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:08.620
<v Paul Davidson>She can get corn, or she's trying to get it, but she has to stand up high for it.
<v Paul Davidson>她能得到玉米，或者她试图得到它，但她必须站得很高才能得到。

00:53:08.620 --> 00:53:10.910
<v Laura Davidson>Is that where you want her to stand?
<v Laura Davidson>那是你想让她站的地方吗？

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:33.370
<v Ira Glass>Someone during this ordeal, a funny thing happens. All the Davidsons, who all started off skeptical, they are completely engaged. Dick suggests a pose that is pure art concept, a pose that could not be further from Standard of Perfection. Laura lures the bird with corn, Paul smooths feathers, and when the bird quivers or moves a wing, three people jump in to fix it back up.
<v Ira Glass>在这个折磨人的过程中，发生了一件有趣的事。所有一开始持怀疑态度的戴维森一家，他们完全投入了。迪克建议了一个纯艺术概念的姿势，一个离《完美标准》最远的姿势。劳拉用玉米诱引这只鸟，保罗梳理羽毛，当鸟颤抖或移动翅膀时，三个人冲进去把它修好。

00:53:33.370 --> 00:53:45.880
<v Tamara Staples>There's some feathers on the breast a little bit. A little bit fluffy. You know, it's like she's not real cleaned on there-- OK. She's a little farther. You guys are a great team. I'm going to hire you to come with me.
<v Tamara Staples>胸部有一些羽毛。有一点蓬松。你知道，就像她没在那儿清理干净一样——好的。她再远一点。你们是一个很棒的团队。我要雇你们和我一起去。

00:53:45.880 --> 00:53:47.740
<v Tamara Staples>Oops, I got a hand in there.
<v Tamara Staples>噢，我把手拍进去了。

00:53:47.740 --> 00:53:49.160
<v Tamara Staples>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Tamara Staples>[说话声重叠]

00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:49.410
<v Tamara Staples>[LAUGHING]
<v Tamara Staples>[笑]

00:53:49.410 --> 00:53:52.570
<v Tamara Staples>Move the hand, move the hand. Move the hand. OK, great.
<v Tamara Staples>移开手，移开手。移开手。好的，太棒了。

00:53:52.570 --> 00:54:11.430
<v Ira Glass>It wasn't until this point that I realized that I came into this sort of expecting the bird to be more, well, more human. Partly, I think, because I had never really thought about this one way or the other. But partly because Tamara's photos make chicken seem so thoughtful.
<v Ira Glass>直到这一点我才意识到，我来这里本来有点期待这只鸟能更……嗯，更人性化。一部分是因为我从来没想过这件事。但部分是因为塔玛拉的照片让鸡看起来很深思熟虑。

00:54:11.430 --> 00:54:12.230
<v Laura Davidson>[UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Laura Davidson>[听不清]。

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:13.200
<v Laura Davidson>[PEOPLE CLICKING TO GET BIRD'S ATTENTION]
<v Laura Davidson>[人们打响指以引起鸟的注意]

00:54:13.200 --> 00:54:16.130
<v Paul Davidson>Over here. Look at the camera, look at the camera. Right there.
<v Paul Davidson>这边。看相机，看相机。就在那儿。

00:54:16.130 --> 00:54:17.110
<v Tamara Staples>No, she's completely out of frame.
<v Tamara Staples>不，她完全在画面外。

00:54:17.110 --> 00:54:20.640
<v Ira Glass>Those photos are a lie.
<v Ira Glass>那些照片是个谎言。

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:22.140
<v Ira Glass>[PEOPLE CLICKING TO GET BIRD'S ATTENTION]
<v Ira Glass>[人们打响指以引起鸟的注意]

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:23.140
<v Tamara Staples>Hello?
<v Tamara Staples>喂？

00:54:23.140 --> 00:54:28.050
<v Paul Davidson>I think you're going to have a one shot opportunity here. It's going to be when I let go.
<v Paul Davidson>我想你在这儿有一个机会，只有一次机会。那将是我放手的时候。

00:54:28.050 --> 00:54:29.790
<v Paul Davidson>[CHICKEN CLUCKING]
<v Paul Davidson>[鸡的咯咯声]

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:30.760
<v Paul Davidson>[WINGS FLUTTERING]
<v Paul Davidson>[翅膀拍打声]

00:54:30.760 --> 00:54:36.490
<v Paul Davidson>Whoa. Geez, I didn't even let go. I just started to let up and he yanked it right out of my hand.
<v Paul Davidson>哇。天哪，我甚至还没放手。我只是开始松手，它就把它从我手里拽出去了。

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:52.710
<v Ira Glass>Fact is, you can try to give chickens respect. You can try to treat them dignity, and photograph them the way you'd photograph anything or anyone that's serious, but the chickens will not care. You can make them look dignified, but it is a brainless, bird-like dignity, and it is ephemeral.
<v Ira Glass>事实是，你可以尝试给鸡尊重。你可以尝试对待它们有尊严，并像拍摄任何严肃的人或物一样拍摄它们，但鸡并不在乎。你可以让它们看起来有尊严，但那是一种无脑的、鸟一样的尊严，而且是短暂的。

00:54:52.710 --> 00:55:00.380
<v Ira Glass>Do you feel like your relationship with chicken has changed because of this?
<v Ira Glass>你觉得你和鸡的关系因为这个改变了吗？

00:55:00.380 --> 00:55:03.590
<v Tamara Staples>No. Not at all.
<v Tamara Staples>不。完全没有。

00:55:03.590 --> 00:55:09.900
<v Ira Glass>How could that not be so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么会这样呢？

00:55:09.900 --> 00:55:15.730
<v Tamara Staples>Um, I order the chicken when I'm at the show. I eat it right in front of the chickens.
<v Tamara Staples>嗯，我在展示的时候点鸡肉吃。我就在鸡面前吃它。

00:55:15.730 --> 00:55:19.160
<v Ira Glass>You eat chicken while you're standing there with a chicken?
<v Ira Glass>你站在那儿和一只鸡在一起时吃鸡肉？

00:55:19.160 --> 00:55:28.300
<v Tamara Staples>Yes. [LAUGHING] Is it wrong? I don't know. I'm hungry.
<v Tamara Staples>是的。[笑] 这错了吗？我不知道。我饿了。

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:32.630
<v Ira Glass>Well, no wonder they won't sit still.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，难怪它们坐不住。

00:55:32.630 --> 00:55:33.590
<v Tamara Staples>Yeah.
<v Tamara Staples>是的。

00:55:33.590 --> 00:55:42.460
<v Tamara Staples>[CHICKING CALLING]
<v Tamara Staples>[鸡的呼叫声]

00:55:42.460 --> 00:56:09.020
<v Ira Glass>We pack up our gear and move the massive wall of hay back into place. As we do this, chickens hop by. Brahmas, Americanas, mixed breeds. They seem utterly uninterested in us. They cluck at each other, there's feed to eat, hay to nestle in-- they have better things to do with their time. And you know, there's nothing that makes you realize just how inhuman chickens are than spending a day trying to make them seem human.
<v Ira Glass>我们收拾好装备，把那堵巨大的干草墙移回原处。当我们做这件事时，鸡跳了过来。婆罗门鸡、美国鸡、杂交鸡。它们对我们完全没兴趣。它们互相咯咯叫，有饲料吃，有干草可以筑巢——它们有更重要的事情要做。你知道，没有什么比花一整天试图让鸡看起来像人更能让你意识到鸡是多么不像人了。

00:56:09.020 --> 00:57:00.330
<v Ira Glass>[CHICKEN AND COW SOUNDS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸡和牛的声音]

00:57:00.330 --> 00:57:14.120
<v Ira Glass>Well, the stories in today's program were produced by Alex Blumberg, Susan Burton, Blue Chevigny, Julie Snyder, Alix Spiegel and Nancy Updike. Musical help from Mr. John Connors. Thanks also to Larry Josephson and Jay Hedblade. Elizabeth Meister runs our web site.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，今天节目中的故事由亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、苏珊·伯顿、蓝色·切维尼、朱莉·斯奈德、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔和南希·厄普代克制作。来自约翰·康纳斯先生的音乐帮助。也感谢拉里·约瑟夫森和杰伊·赫德布莱德。伊丽莎白·迈斯特负责我们的网站。

00:57:14.120 --> 00:58:20.010
<v Ira Glass>Tamara Staples' photographs of chickens are now in a book called Fairest Fowl: Portraits of Championship Chickens. Susan Vitucci's opera about Chicken Little is available on CD at www.pulcina.org. That is Pulcina spelled, of course, P-U-L-C-I-N-A.
<v Ira Glass>塔玛拉·斯台普斯的鸡的照片现在被收录在一本名为《最美的家禽：冠军鸡的肖像》的书里。苏珊·维图奇关于鸡丁的歌剧可以在www.pulcina.org网站上以CD形式购买。当然，“Pulcina”的拼写是P-U-L-C-I-N-A。

00:58:20.010 --> 00:58:26.730
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who decided he did not want to come onto our program after he asked just one question--
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ的管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责，他决定在问了一个问题后不想参加我们的节目——

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:29.430
<v Mrs. Mattoon>This is just radio, not TV?
<v Mrs. Mattoon>这只是广播，不是电视？

00:58:29.430 --> 00:58:55.440
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周再见，会有更多《这个美国生活》的故事。

00:58:55.440 --> 00:59:00.440
<v Announcer>PRI. Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI。国际公共广播电台。
